<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warpalawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Warpalawas, Urballa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Warpalawa was king of Tuwana, a Neo-Hittite kingdom in southern Cappadocia, in the second half of the 8th century BCE. Assyrian records name him as Urballa among the rulers who paid tribute to Tiglath-Pileser III. He left inscriptions in Luwian hieroglyphs and is shown on the famous rock relief at İvriz worshipping the storm god Tarhunza. After his reign Tuwana fades from the record.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warpalawa.jpg" alt="Warpalawa" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warpalawas,_king_of_Tyana,_prays_in_front_of_divine_symbols._Detail_of_a_stele_from_Bor._8th_century_BC._Museum_of_the_Ancient_Orient,_Istanbul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Warpalawa, Warpallawas in Luwian, was the son of the previous king of Tuwana, Muwaḫḫaranis I, whom he succeeded around 740 BCE. Father and son may have belonged to a dynasty that ruled Tuwana for much of the 8th century BCE, and an earlier king of the same name, Warpalawas I, may have been their ancestor. The name comes from the Luwian adjective warpallas, mighty or powerful. The linguist Ilya Yakubovich reads it as royal warrior, while Rostyslav Oreshko takes warpallas as an epithet of the storm god Tarḫunzas, making the name mean One of the Powerful.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Assyrian records name him, as Urpallâ, among five kings who brought tribute to Tiglath-Pileser III in 738 and 737 BCE, and he seems to have kept to a policy of cooperation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. His monuments show it: on the relief at Ivriz and on the Bor stele he is carved in an Assyrianising style. Tuwana was strong enough to hold a sub-kingdom at the site of present-day Porsuk, whose king Tarḫunazzas called himself Warpalawa's servant and received Mount Mudi, near the Cilician Gates, as a reward. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasusarma">Wasusarmas</a> of Tabal claimed his help in a war against a coalition led by the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, though this is uncertain.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambaris">Ambaris</a> of Tabal was deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> in 713 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> placed the region under the governor Aššur-šarru-uṣur. Warpalawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awariku">Awarikus</a> of Ḫiyawa, though loyal, were judged too old to uphold Assyrian authority against growing Phrygian power, and became largely symbolic rulers, perhaps still managing their kingdoms locally. Warpalawa ruled part of Bīt-Burutaš, some of whose cities were seized from him by Atuna and Ištuanda.</p><p>Around 710 or 709 BCE Aššur-šarru-uṣur reported that Warpalawa had asked for an audience together with an envoy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, and doubted his loyalty. He is last mentioned around 709 BCE. Old Phrygian inscriptions possibly of his reign, and the robe with Phrygian geometric designs he wears at Ivriz, suggest that Phrygian culture was reaching Tuwana.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Warpalawas II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King <b>Warpalawa</b> ruled with Luwian hieroglyphic inscriptions and is shown on the rock relief at Ivriz before the storm god Tarhunza, who holds grapes and ears of wheat, symbols of the land's fertility.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuwana">Tuwana</a></li></ul></section>
